SUMMARY:[AI ADJUSTED] running — Cap at 8km @ 7:00–7:15/km (55–60 min)
  walk-run with HR stric
DESCRIPTION:Cap at 8km @ 7:00–7:15/km (55–60 min) walk-run with HR stri
 ctly ≤140 bpm regardless of how good you feel\; if body battery on wakin
 g is <55\, replace with 30-min flat walk only.\n\nReason: Given the severe
  sleep disruption through late July\, even meeting the sleep gate does not
  mean full fitness has returned — a more conservative 8km ceiling reduce
 s injury risk and leaves room to build in Wk8 and Wk9.\n\nAI coaching 2026
 -08-01
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai-coaching-20260811-2@jean-tri
DTSTAMP:20260801T031643Z
DTSTART:20260811T070000
DTEND:20260811T090000
SUMMARY:[AI ADJUSTED] running — 30–35 min easy run at 6:45–7:00/km wi
 th HR ≤145 bpm\; include
DESCRIPTION:30–35 min easy run at 6:45–7:00/km with HR ≤145 bpm\; inc
 lude 4 × 20-second strides at ~5:45/km (Treviso race pace) with full walk
  recovery to begin neuromuscular priming without adding load.\n\nReason: W
 ith Treviso 10K 63 days away\, introducing very short race-pace strides wi
 thin an easy run is the most efficient way to start building pace-specific
  adaptation while keeping overall stress low.\n\nAI coaching 2026-08-01
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ai-coaching-20260820-3@jean-tri
DTSTAMP:20260801T031643Z
DTSTART:20260820T070000
DTEND:20260820T090000
SUMMARY:[AI ADJUSTED] running — Keep structure but reduce to 3 × 1km @ 5
 :45/km with 2-min wa
DESCRIPTION:Keep structure but reduce to 3 × 1km @ 5:45/km with 2-min walk
  recovery\; add a strict HR ceiling of 170 bpm during intervals and stop t
 he set if exceeded — extend to 4 reps only if all three complete under 1
 70 bpm and effort feels controlled at 7/10.\n\nReason: Given the training 
 background (no recent interval history\, disrupted sleep block through Jul
 y)\, 3 reps is a safer first interval session than 4 and reduces the risk 
 of the HR spike pattern seen on July 18.\n\nAI coaching 2026-08-01
END:VEVENT
